---
source: crates/acpilot-cli/tests/cli.rs
expression: stdout(&serve)
---
Serve the daemon and the web UI over HTTP, printing its address and token.

One process, serving the same state `/v1` answers from: a browser at the printed URL talks to the
daemon a TUI would attach to, not to a copy of it. Every request needs `Authorization: Bearer
<token>` except the page itself, which a browser cannot give a header to when the address is typed.

A loopback port is picked by the operating system unless `--bind` names one, and a token is
generated when `--token` does not supply one. Both are printed when the listener is up, in one
panel, because a URL without the token stops at the page's token field and a token without the port
names nothing.

Usage: acpilot serve [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --profile <NAME|DIR>
          Profile name, or a profile directory.
          
          Accepted before or after the subcommand, because every verb that has an opinion about
          identity reads one choice. A single segment is a profile name under `<config>/profiles`; a
          path opens that directory. `tui` and `serve` use it only when they start the daemon: a
          daemon already running keeps the profile it was started with, and naming a different one
          there is an error rather than a silent mismatch. A name that does not resolve is an error,
          never a quieter fallback: guessing sends a work prompt with a personal key.
          
          [env: ACPILOT_PROFILE=]

      --bind <ADDR>
          The address to bind, for example `0.0.0.0:7777`.
          
          Without it the OS picks a free loopback port and the panel prints it.

      --token <TOKEN>
          Use this bearer token instead of generating one.
          
          It is written to the runtime token file the daemon reads. Without it a token is generated
          on first use and reused, so a browser keeps working across restarts.

      --workspace <DIR>
          Workspace root the daemon serves. Defaults to this directory

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
